# DEFERRED-GAP FIXTURE (#[ignore]'d — the self-host OVER-ACCEPTS an unknown
# scheduler mode and silently drops it; see todo/t0825).
#
# Rust gg REJECTS `directive scheduler=bogus` with `error[E_UnknownDirective]`
# (`src/semantic/mod.rs`; pinned on that lane by
# `tests/fixtures/unknown_scheduler_mode_error.gg`). The self-host performs NO
# directive validation at all — `self_host_check/resolve.gg`'s `IDirective` arm
# is a bare `pass`, and no self-host source mentions `E_UnknownDirective` — so it
# accepts the program, and `self_host_lowerer/lower.gg`'s scheduler arm has no
# `else`, so the value is silently dropped and the module keeps
# `new_lir_module`'s default. That is Core #10's lower-or-reject in its exact
# forbidden shape: user syntax discarded with no diagnostic.
#
# It is NOT cosmetic, because the dropped value changes observable behaviour.
# MEASURED 2026-08-30 on the self-host lane, emit + cc + run of THIS program,
# rc 0 at every stage, against the same program with `scheduler=single`:
#   scheduler=bogus   -> `// ── Scheduler: Pool (M:N thread pool …) ──`
#                        worker runs | after sleep | 1 | 1
#   scheduler=single  -> `// ── Scheduler: Single (N:1 cooperative loop) ──`
#                        after sleep | 0 | worker runs | 1
# So a typo in the mode silently changes an async program's interleaving.
#
# The INTENDED state is a REJECTION on this lane, matching Rust gg — which is
# what the `#[ignore]`d `sh_rejects_unknown_scheduler_mode` asserts, and why it
# is RED at HEAD. Un-ignore it and promote this fixture out of `known_gaps/` the
# round the self-host validates directives; at that point
# `unknown_scheduler_mode_error.gg` becomes a two-lane pin.
#
# ⚠ The `sleep` here is deliberate: it is what makes the two schedulers
# distinguishable on stdout. That `sleep` is not a scheduling point under
# `single` is a SEPARATE filed gap (todo/t0822) and does not affect this one —
# either way the two modes differ, which is the property under test.

directive scheduler=bogus

from std.time import sleep

async void worker(int &counter):
    print("worker runs")
    counter = counter + 1

async void main():
    shared int x = 0
    Task[void] t = spawn worker(&x)
    sleep(0.1)
    print("after sleep")
    with x:
        print(x)
    t.await()
    print(x)
